        "prefix_token_count": 25,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Identify the number of squares of each size that can be formed on a $3 \\times n$ grid."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 307,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 308,
        "char_start": 96,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 83,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 84,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Calculate the number of $1 \\times 1$ squares. For any $3 \\times n$ grid, there are $(n-1) \\times 3$ such squares because you can choose any two adjacent rows and any two adjacent columns to form a square."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 477,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 478,
        "char_start": 310,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 136,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 137,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Calculate the number of $2 \\times 2$ squares. For these, you need at least 2 columns and 2 rows to form a square. Thus, there are $(n-2) \\times 2$ such squares."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 620,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 621,
        "char_start": 480,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 177,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 178,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Calculate the number of $3 \\times 3$ squares. These require at least 3 columns and 3 rows. Therefore, there are $(n-3)$ such squares."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 774,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 775,
        "char_start": 623,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 234,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 235,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Sum up all the squares. The total number of squares is given by the sum of squares of each size: $(n-1) \\times 3 + (n-2) \\times 2 + (n-3) = 70$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 838,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 839,
        "char_start": 777,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 268,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 269,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: Simplify the equation: $3(n-1) + 2(n-2) + (n-3) = 70$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 910,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 911,
        "char_start": 841,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 302,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 303,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Expand and combine like terms: $3n - 3 + 2n - 4 + n - 3 = 70$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 954,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 955,
        "char_start": 913,
        "effective_step": 8,
        "end_token_index": 323,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 324,
        "step_number": 8,
        "text": "Step 8: Combine the terms: $6n - 10 = 70$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1029,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1030,
        "char_start": 957,
        "effective_step": 9,
        "end_token_index": 362,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 363,
        "step_number": 9,
        "text": "Step 9: Solve for $n$: $6n = 80$, thus $n = \\frac{80}{6} = \\frac{40}{3}$."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1169,
